The query is doing a sequential scan over the entire `orders` table,
filtering by `customer_id`, then sorting the matching rows by
`created_at` and returning the top 10. With a large `orders` table,
this gets very slow.

The right index here is a composite covering both columns in the
right order:

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_created
  ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC);
```

Why this order: PostgreSQL/MySQL/etc. will use the index to seek to
the customer's rows directly (the equality predicate goes first),
and because `created_at DESC` is part of the index, it can read the
top 10 in order without a separate sort step.

Trade-off: every INSERT/UPDATE on `orders` now has to maintain this
index, which adds a small amount of write overhead. On a write-heavy
table this can matter; on a read-heavy table it's negligible.
